  "worked_solution": "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function is not left-continuous because it's undefined at this point. $\\,\\times $\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=2=f(1).\\,\u2713\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=0=f(3).\\,\u2713\n$$\n\nTherefore, from **left to right**, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at $x=1$ and $x=3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
